# What can one candle do? La Tour's Magdalen with the Smoking Flame
tags: ["art history","caravaggio lighting","renaissance painting","symbolism","memento mori","baroque"]
teaches: ["art-history/baroque-light-techniques","interpretation/religious-symbolism","visual-analysis/composition","concepts/memento-mori"]
thumb: "none picture \"File:Georges de La Tour - The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame - Google Art Project.jpg\" colour"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, knowledgeable gallery guide, quietly amused","b":"gemini:Puck | a relaxed, good-humoured visitor with a dry, understated wit; curious, never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What is lit, and what is hidden? Why a candle and a skull?"
next: {"title":"Why does window light feel so calm? Vermeer's The Milkmaid","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/how-painters-caught-light/05-why-does-window-light-feel-so-calm.cast"}

## The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame: one small light
sources:
    - id: lacma
      title: The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame (M.77.73)
      authors: Los Angeles County Museum of Art
      year: 2026
      finding: Georges de La Tour, c. 1635–37, oil on canvas, 117 × 91.76 cm; the flame is the chief symbol and true subject; skull, Scripture and scourge set the mood; the light linked to St John of the Cross's 'living flame of love'
      url: https://collections.lacma.org/object/30854
    - id: wiki
      title: Magdalene with the Smoking Flame
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Two versions, Louvre and LACMA; skull on her lap, books, cross and rope scourge on the table, a rope at her waist
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdalene_with_the_Smoking_Flame
    - id: latour
      title: Georges de La Tour
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: 1593–1652, worked in Lunéville in the Duchy of Lorraine; known for candlelit night scenes; forgotten until rediscovered by Hermann Voss in 1915
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georges_de_La_Tour

    card title "The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame"

B: A woman alone at night, a skull, and one small flame. What can one flame do?
    image art cites ["lacma","wiki"] credit "Georges de La Tour, c. 1635–37. Los Angeles County Museum of Art. Public domain." x 290 y 334 width 500 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Georges_de_La_Tour_-_The_Magdalen_with_the_Smoking_Flame_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Georges_de_La_Tour_-_The_Magdalen_with_the_Smoking_Flame_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.blouse [0.15,0.22,0.41,0.24] regions.books [0.725,0.305,0.2,0.1] regions.cross [0.72,0.415,0.27,0.045] regions.dark_corner [0.76,0.47,0.24,0.42] regions.eye [0.44,0.18,0.03,0.03] regions.face [0.38,0.14,0.095,0.11] regions.figure [0.05,0.04,0.7,0.94] regions.flame [0.643,0.235,0.034,0.08] regions.flame_up [0.6,0.1,0.11,0.22] regions.glass [0.595,0.305,0.13,0.145] regions.lamp [0.59,0.22,0.14,0.23] regions.legs [0.3,0.58,0.62,0.42] regions.lit_pool [0.15,0.07,0.6,0.4] regions.lower_dark [0,0.6,1,0.4] regions.scourge [0.73,0.395,0.17,0.035] regions.skull [0.455,0.475,0.16,0.11] regions.smoke [0.625,0.09,0.065,0.155] regions.table [0.55,0.3,0.45,0.17] regions.waist_rope [0.24,0.435,0.24,0.05]

A: This is Georges de La Tour's Magdalen with the Smoking Flame, painted in Lorraine in the 1630s. He painted her by night more than once; this version hangs in Los Angeles.
    camera on ["art@[0.1,0.04,0.82,0.6]"] duration 6

B: And this time the light is right there on the table. Caravaggio's beam came from somewhere off the edge.
    point at.ref art:lamp gesture glow

A: That's the new step: the source sits inside the picture, and one small flame lights everything we see.
    highlight art:flame strength strong lift true

    pause 0.8

    camera reset true

B: A candle, I suppose.
    highlight art:lamp effect detail lift true

A: Look closer: a wick in oil, in a glass, a little night lamp, with a thread of black smoke rising off the flame.
    highlight art:glass art:smoke effect detail

    camera reset true

B: So what does it light? Her face, her shoulder, that white blouse.
    highlight art:face art:blouse

A: Whatever is near it, and the  (@highlight art:flame art:blouse art:legs lift true@)nearer, the brighter: a small flame's light fades fast with distance.

B: And further down, she just disappears.
    point at.ref art:legs gesture glow

A: Her legs, the floor, the corner sink int (@highlight art:lit_pool strength strong lift true@)o the dark. La Tour lets the room end where the light ends.

    pause 0.8

B: She's staring at the skull in her lap, surely.
    point at.ref art:skull gesture glow

    ask "Follow her eyes. Tap what she is looking at." spot lamp on art voice a
        right: Yes: the flame. Her hand rests on the skull, but her eyes stay on the light.
        wrong: Follow the line from her eyes, out past her hand and across the table.

    camera on ["art@[0.33,0.08,0.45,0.55]"]

    point at.ref art:flame gesture arrow

    highlight art:flame strength strong lift true duration 1.5

    camera reset true

B: Why a skull, though?
    highlight art:skull effect detail lift true

A: It's a memento mori, a reminder that everyone dies. And in the tradition of La Tour's time, the Magdalen was the sinner who gave up her old life to repent.
    highlight art:skull effect detail

B: And that rope round her waist? Part of it?
    highlight art:waist_rope effect detail lift true

A: Yes: the rope, and the knotte (@highlight art:scourge effect detail lift true@)d scourge on the table, are the tools of her penance.

B: And the flame is burning itself away while she watches.
    highlight art:flame_up effect detail lift true

A: That's the old meaning: a flame that uses itself up is a short life, and its smoke drifts off and is gone.
    highlight art:flame_up art:smoke effect detail

B: That's rather gloomy.
    highlight art:smoke strength soft

    camera reset true

A: There's a brighter reading too. The museum links the flame to the living flame of love of the mystic Saint John of the Cross, a light that leads the soul out of its dark night.
    highlight art:flame lift true

B: So the skull is what she holds, and the flame is what she thinks about.
    highlight art:skull art:flame

    camera on ["art@[0.25,0.04,0.55,0.5]"]

A: And one flame does both jobs: it lights her face, and it is what her face is turned to.
    focus art:face art:flame strength strong rest grey

    pause 0.8

    camera reset true

    quiz "A stage director wants a scene where a man sits alone at night, deciding whether to forgive his brother. Which lighting follows La Tour?" voice a
        right: The lamp beside him: the light sits in the scene with him, shows only what is near, and gives him something to stare into.
        wrong: Where is La Tour's light: outside the room, or in it?
        * A hard beam from one high window
        * Soft daylight from a side window
        * Even light from all around
        + A lamp beside him that he stares into

B: Every light so far came in from outside. This one sits on the table with her, and waits.
    highlight art:flame strength soft lift true

